From 312bc6dfa4b4deaab39ec746c67343268de44845 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 04:16:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(gateway): watch-notice fixture waits out the inbound
 turn's drain [daveey]

tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py::test_notice_pinned_to_a_compression_ancestor_still_admits
failed in CI (run 43771307563, job 132987778881, slice 5/8) with
`assert ['started'] == ['queued']`: the notice was admitted, but already claimed.

Five whys:
1. Why was the admitted row 'started'? A drain claimed it.
2. Why did a drain run when `_gateway()` patched `SessionAuthority._schedule` to a no-op?
   The inbound turn's own drain was still alive: patching `_schedule` stops new drains,
   not the one already running.
3. Why was it alive after the fixture saw the turn's rows terminal? `_drain_rows` settles
   the row and then loops to re-read the FIFO (`claim_session_input`, off-loop) before it
   exits idle; `_settled` only waits for the terminal row.
4. Why did that re-read take the notice? Its read raced the notice's admission: with
   `head=None` it saw a new queued row, returned False ("re-read"), and the next pass
   claimed the notice.
5. Why only sometimes? On a loaded runner the off-loop re-read was still pending when
   the test admitted the notice; usually it has finished first.

Fix (test only): after the settle wait, `_gateway()` awaits every live drain task
(bounded like the settle wait) before patching `_schedule`, so no drain from the
setup turn can claim what a test admits. Product code unchanged; the
'admitted and queued' assertion is unchanged. Covers every test in the file using
`_gateway`.

Proof (worker-7): a scratch probe holds the drain's post-settlement
`claim_session_input` until the notice is admitted (harness-only bound).
RED against main's fixture: rows `[(<parent>, 'started')]`, the incident symptom,
on both of the runner's attempts. GREEN with this fixture: passes. Whole file 9 passed x3
(11.3-11.7 s, same as main's 11.6 s locally).
---
 tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py b/tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py
index a4f98a5a97f07ae0c8ab400b15b5c0037f30cf50..53420b3f34df0e1fc3b4c0d996a3db793bef186d 100644
--- a/tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py
+++ b/tests/gateway/test_watch_notification_reset_pin.py
@@ -44,6 +44,10 @@ async def _gateway(monkeypatch):
     key = runner.session_store._generate_session_key(source)
     await async_wait_until(lambda: _settled(authority, runner.session_store.peek_session_id(key)),
                            timeout=15, what='the inbound turn to settle')
+    # The turn's drain outlives its settlement (it re-reads the FIFO before exiting idle): a drain
+    # still alive here would claim the notice a test admits next, whatever ``_schedule`` does.
+    await asyncio.wait_for(asyncio.gather(
+        *[live.task for live in authority.sessions.values() if live.task is not None]), 15)
     # Admitted notices stay queued from here on: these tests assert the admission, not its turn.
     monkeypatch.setattr(SessionAuthority, '_schedule', lambda self, ref: None)
     return runner, authority, runner.session_store.lookup_by_session_key(key)
